    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    giants = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[1 + 2*i])\n        b = int(data[1 + 2*i + 1])\n        giants.append((a, b))\n    \n    # To maximize the total height, we need to determine the optimal order to stack the giants\n    # Key insight: For two adjacent giants i and j (with i below j), \n    # it's better to put i before j if A_i - B_i < A_j - B_j\n    # This is because the shoulder height of the next giant is what matters for stacking,\n    # and we want to minimize the \"loss\" from the previous giant's head height\n    \n    # Sort giants by (A_i - B_i) in ascending order\n    giants.sort(key=lambda x: x[0] - x[1])\n    \n    # Calculate total height\n    total_height = 0\n    for a, b in giants:\n        total_height += a  # Add shoulder height\n    \n    # Add the head height of the top giant\n    # Since we've stacked them in sorted order, the last giant in our list is the top one\n    _, head_height = giants[-1]\n    total_height += head_height - giants[-1][0]  # head_height = a + (b - a) => just add (b - a)\n    \n    print(total_height)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
