    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge is an undirected edge connecting vertices u_i and v_i.\nA graph with N vertices is called good if the following condition holds for all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K:\n\n- there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i in G.\n\nThe given graph G is good.\nYou are given Q independent questions. Answer all of them.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th question is as follows.\n\n- Is the graph G^{{(i)}} obtained by adding an undirected edge connecting vertices p_i and q_i to the given graph G good?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nu_1 v_1\r\nu_2 v_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nu_M v_M\r\nK\r\nx_1 y_1\r\nx_2 y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_K y_K\r\nQ\r\np_1 q_1\r\np_2 q_2\r\n\\vdots\r\np_Q q_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th line should contain the answer to the i-th question: Yes if the graph G^{{(i)}} is good, and No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq M \\leq 2 \\times10^5\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq x_i, y_i \\leq N\n- x_i \\neq y_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace x_i, y_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace x_j, y_j \\rbrace\n- For all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K, there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i.\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- p_i \\neq q_i\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 3\r\n3 1\r\n5 4\r\n5 5\r\n3\r\n1 5\r\n2 6\r\n4 3\r\n4\r\n2 5\r\n2 6\r\n5 6\r\n5 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\nNo\r\nNo\r\nYes\r\nYes\r\n\n\n- For the first question, the graph G^{{(1)}} is not good because it has a path 1 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 5 connecting vertices x_1 = 1 and y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to process a large graph efficiently (N, M, K, Q up to 2×10^5).  \n\nThe key points from the problem:\n\n1. The original graph is **good**, meaning for each of the K forbidden pairs `(x_i, y_i)`, there is **no path** between them in the original graph.\n2. When we add a new edge `(p, q)`, we must check if it creates a path for any forbidden pair.\n3. If adding `(p, q)` connects two vertices that were already connected, it doesn't change connectivity between other pairs, so it can't make a forbidden pair connected unless `(p, q)` is itself a forbidden pair's endpoints.\n4. If adding `(p, q)` connects two previously disconnected components, we must check if any forbidden pair had endpoints in those two components — because then they become connected.\n\n**Efficient approach:**\n- Use DSU (Union-Find) to manage connected components of the original graph.\n- For each forbidden pair `(x_i, y_i)`, they must be in **different components** in the original graph. Store for each component pair `(root(x), root(y))` the fact that this connection is forbidden.\n- When adding a new edge `(p, q)`:\n  - If `p` and `q` are already in the same component → adding the edge does nothing to connectivity, so the graph remains good **unless** `(p, q)` itself is a forbidden pair (but since original graph is good, `(p, q)` can't be forbidden unless they were already connected? Wait — forbidden pairs can be in the same component? No, forbidden pairs have no path, so they must be in different components in original graph. So `(p, q)` can't be a forbidden pair if they are already connected in original graph, because then they'd have a path. So if they are in same component, it's fine.)\n  - If `p` and `q` are in different components, merging them is allowed **only if** there is no forbidden pair between these two components. So we need a quick lookup: is `(root(p), root(q))` a forbidden component pair?\n\nBut storing all forbidden component pairs directly in a set is fine: K ≤ 2×10^5, so we store them as sorted tuples `(min(root(x), root(y)), max(root(x), root(y)))`.\n\n**Complexities:**\n- DSU operations: near O(1).\n- Lookup in set: O(1).\n\n**Edge cases:**\n- Multiple edges, self-loops: DSU handles them fine (self-loops don't change connectivity).\n- Forbidden pairs are unique, but multiple forbidden pairs might map to same component pair.\n\n**Implementation plan:**\n1. Read N, M, build DSU from M edges.\n2. Read K forbidden pairs, for each find roots in DSU, store in a set as sorted tuple (smaller root first).\n3. Read Q queries, for each `(p, q)`:\n   - Find roots of p, q.\n   - If root(p) == root(q) → answer \"Yes\".\n   - Else, if `(min(root(p), root(q)), max(root(p), root(q)))` is in forbidden set → answer \"No\".\n   - Else → \"Yes\".\n\nLet's implement.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(1 << 25)\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    \n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    \n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    size = [1] * (N + 1)\n    \n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    \n    def union(a, b):\n        ra, rb = find(a), find(b)\n        if ra == rb:\n            return\n        if size[ra] < size[rb]:\n            ra, rb = rb, ra\n        parent[rb] = ra\n        size[ra] += size[rb]\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it))\n        v = int(next(it))\n        union(u, v)\n    \n    K = int(next(it))\n    forbidden = set()\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(next(it))\n        y = int(next(it))\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx > ry:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        forbidden.add((rx, ry))\n    \n    Q = int(next(it))\n    out_lines = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(next(it))\n        q = int(next(it))\n        rp, rq = find(p), find(q)\n        if rp > rq:\n            rp, rq = rq, rp\n        if (rp, rq) in forbidden:\n            out_lines.append(\"No\")\n        else:\n            out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out_lines))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
