      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef modinv(a):\n    return pow(a, MOD - 2, MOD)\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split('\\n')\n    if not data: \n        return\n    n = int(data[0])\n    matches = []\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        p, q = map(int, data[i].split())\n        matches.append((p-1, q-1))  # 0-indexed\n    \n    # We'll process matches in reverse order\n    # For each match, we combine two components and update the expected win counts\n    # Using linearity of expectation and backward DP\n    \n    # parent array for union-find (to track component sizes)\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    size = [1] * n\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return rx\n        if size[rx] < size[ry]:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        parent[ry] = rx\n        size[rx] += size[ry]\n        return rx\n    \n    # ans[i]: expected number of wins for player i\n    ans = [0] * n\n    \n    # Process matches in reverse order\n    for i in range(n-2, -1, -1):\n        p, q = matches[i]\n        rp, rq = find(p), find(q)\n        sp, sq = size[rp], size[rq]\n        # In the current state (before match i), components have sizes sp and sq\n        # The match combines them: \n        #   The probability that the team with p wins is sp/(sp+sq)\n        #   The probability that the team with q wins is sq/(sp+sq)\n        # \n        # After match i, all players in the combined component will have their future expected wins increased\n        # But crucially: when the component containing p wins, ALL players in p's component get +1 for this win.\n        # Similarly for q's component.\n        \n        # So for each player in p's component, we add: Pr(team p wins) * 1 = sp/(sp+sq)\n        # For each player in q's component, we add: Pr(team q wins) * 1 = sq/(sp+sq)\n        \n        win_p = sp * modinv(sp + sq) % MOD\n        win_q = sq * modinv(sp + sq) % MOD\n        \n        # Add win_p to every player in rp's component, win_q to every player in rq's component\n        # We'll do lazy propagation - we only need the final answers\n        # Instead of iterating over all nodes (too slow), we'll use an auxiliary array and accumulate contributions\n        \n        # Alternative approach: maintain an array \"contrib\" where contrib[root] is the total contribution \n        # to add to each node in that component (when we get to the root). But since we're processing backwards,\n        # we can instead push contributions upward.\n        \n        # Actually, simpler: maintain ans, and for each component, keep track of how much each node in it should get from future operations.\n        # But we're going backwards, so let's track additive updates per component.\n        \n        # We'll store for each root, the amount that each node in its component should get\n        # However, after union, the new component gets:\n        #   For old rp component: they get win_p now, plus whatever future contributions (from more reversed matches)\n        #   For old rq component: they get win_q now, plus whatever future contributions\n        #\n        # So during reverse processing, we maintain:\n        #   val[root] = additional value to add to each node in component with root\n        \n        # Let's initialize val = [0]*n\n        # But we haven't done that yet. Let me refactor.\n        pass\n\n    # Revised plan:\n    #   Initialize:\n    #       parent, size as above\n    #       comp_val[root] = accumulated value to add to each node in component (initially 0)\n    #   For match i from last to first:\n    #       rp, rq = find(p), find(q)\n    #       sp, sq = size[rp], size[rq]\n    #       win_p = sp / (sp+sq)\n    #       win_q = sq / (sp+sq)\n    #       \n    #       Then, we add win_p to every node in component rp, win_q to every node in component rq.\n    #       Which means: comp_val[rp] += win_p, comp_val[rq] += win_q\n    #       But wait: actually, the win happens NOW, so all players in rp get +win_p, all in rq get +win_q.\n    #       Then they merge. So for the new component, we want to remember that each node gets comp_val[rp] (which now includes win_p) \n    #       but we also need to propagate comp_val[rp] to all nodes when we finally compute ans.\n    #       \n    #   However, if we just store comp_val[root], then at the end, for each node, we can traverse up the tree and sum comp_val along the path.\n    #   But that's O(n log n) worst-case, which might be acceptable? But we want O(n α(n)).\n    #   \n    #   Better: when we union, we don't immediately update all nodes, but we can accumulate in a way that when we find the root, we know the total addition.\n    #   Actually, let's just do path compression and store the extra value at root.\n    #\n    #   Alternate efficient method:\n    #       Let f[i] = total expected wins for player i.\n    #       Process matches from last to first (reverse order), and for each match (p,q):\n    #           size of p's component: sp, q's component: sq.\n    #           Then: f[i] for i in p's component gets += sp/(sp+sq)\n    #                 f[i] for i in q's component gets += sq/(sp+sq)\n    #           Then merge the components.\n    #\n    #   To do this efficiently, we can maintain an array \"add\", and when merging, push the additive updates.\n    #   But pushing to every element is O(n). Instead, use union-find with lazy value storage.\n    #\n    #   We'll maintain an array \"extra\" of length n, where extra[r] is the total amount that each node in component r should get.\n    #   When we merge rp and rq into new root (say rp), then:\n    #       extra[rp] = (extra[rp] + win_p)   # but wait, extra[rp] was already the accumulated value for rp's component from later matches\n    #       extra[rq] = (ex