    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s consisting of only lowercase English letters. In one operation, you can do the following:\n\nSelect any non-empty substring of s, possibly the entire string, then replace each one of its characters with the previous character of the English alphabet. For example, 'b' is converted to 'a', and 'a' is converted to 'z'.\n\nReturn the lexicographically smallest string you can obtain after performing the above operation exactly once.\nA substring is a contiguous sequence of characters in a string.\nA string x is lexicographically smaller than a string y of the same length if x[i] comes before y[i] in alphabetic order for the first position i such that x[i] != y[i].\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"cbabc\"\nOutput: \"baabc\"\nExplanation: We apply the operation on the substring starting at index 0, and ending at index 1 inclusive. \nIt can be proven that the resulting string is the lexicographically smallest. \n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"acbbc\"\nOutput: \"abaab\"\nExplanation: We apply the operation on the substring starting at index 1, and ending at index 4 inclusive. \nIt can be proven that the resulting string is the lexicographically smallest. \n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: s = \"leetcode\"\nOutput: \"kddsbncd\"\nExplanation: We apply the operation on the entire string. \nIt can be proven that the resulting string is the lexicographically smallest. \n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 3 * 10^5\ns consists of lowercase English letters",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef getSmallestString(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the lexicographically smallest string after performing\n    the operation exactly once.\n    \n    The operation: select any non-empty substring and replace each character\n    with the previous character in the alphabet ('a' becomes 'z').\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # If the string starts with 'a', we need to be careful about where we apply the operation\n    # Otherwise, we can just apply it to the entire string or a prefix\n    \n    # Find the first non-'a' character\n    first_non_a = -1\n    for i in range(n):\n        if s[i] != 'a':\n            first_non_a = i\n            break\n    \n    # If all characters are 'a', applying the operation to any substring\n    # will make it lexicographically larger, so we apply to the last character\n    if first_non_a == -1:\n        return s[:-1] + 'z'\n    \n    # If the first character is not 'a', we can apply the operation to the entire string\n    # or to a prefix starting from index 0\n    if first_non_a == 0:\n        # Apply operation to as long a prefix as possible (until we hit an 'a')\n        end_pos = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            if s[i] == 'a':\n                break\n            end_pos = i\n        \n        # Convert substring from 0 to end_pos (inclusive)\n        result = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            if i <= end_pos:\n                # Convert to previous character\n                if s[i] == 'a':\n                    result.append('z')\n                else:\n                    result.append(chr(ord(s[i]) - 1))\n            else:\n                result.append(s[i])\n        return ''.join(result)\n    \n    # If the first non-'a' character is not at the beginning\n    # We have two cases to consider:\n    # 1. Apply operation starting from the first non-'a' character\n    # 2. But we want the lexicographically smallest result\n    \n    # Actually, the optimal strategy is:\n    # - If we have leading 'a's, we should not touch them\n    # - Then find the longest consecutive sequence of non-'a' characters\n    #   and apply the operation to that substring\n    \n    # Find the first block of consecutive non-'a' characters after any leading 'a's\n    start = first_non_a\n    end = start\n    while end + 1 < n and s[end + 1] != 'a':\n        end += 1\n    \n    # Apply operation to this block\n    result = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        if start <= i <= end:\n            # Apply operation (previous character)\n            if s[i] == 'a':\n                result.append('z')\n            else:\n                result.append(chr(ord(s[i]) - 1))\n        else:\n            result.append(s[i])\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
