      "generated_answer": "We are asked to compute the **probability that song 1 is being played at time `X + 0.5` seconds**, under a random-play model where each song is selected uniformly at random (with replacement), and played in full, continuously.\n\nWe are to **output the probability modulo 998244353**, interpreted as the modular inverse of the denominator times the numerator.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 Key Observations\n\n- At time `X + 0.5`, a song is playing if:\n  - The total duration of the sequence of songs played so far is **≤ X**, and\n  - Adding the length of the **current song** makes it **> X** (i.e., the current song is still playing at `X + 0.5`).\n- We are **specifically** interested in the event that the **current song at time X + 0.5 is song 1**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Approach\n\nLet’s use **dynamic programming**:\n\n- Let `dp[t]` be the total **unnormalized** probability (i.e., multiplied by `N^k` for k steps) of reaching time `t` via any sequence of songs, **excluding** those paths where song 1 is currently playing.\n- But instead, we can:\n  - Compute the total **number of ways** (weighted by probability) that we are playing **song 1** at time `X + 0.5`.\n\nBut since probabilities are rational numbers and we are working modulo 998244353, we can think in terms of **expected contribution**, using DP and modular arithmetic.\n\nWe'll use dynamic programming where:\n\n- `dp[t]` = the total **probability mass** (as a rational number, kept modulo 998244353) that time `t` has been reached **just before** starting a new song.\n\nThen, for each time `t ≤ X`, if we then choose song 1 (which takes `T1` seconds), then:\n- if `t <= X` and `t + T1 > X`, then **song 1 is playing at X + 0.5**.\n- The contribution to the probability is: `dp[t] * inv(N)`.\n\nBut the twist is: we are allowed to play any song (including song 1) at any step, except that we only care about transitions **into song 1** that result in song 1 playing at time `X + 0.5`.\n\nSo we can do:\n\n1. Initiali