      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to **reconstruct the initial set of stars** that were used to build the final tree $ T $, based on the described operation.\n\n---\n\n### 🔍 **Understanding the Process**\n\nWe know:\n\n1. Start with a **forest of stars**: a level-$ k $ star has:\n   - $ k+1 $ vertices,\n   - one central vertex connected to $ k $ leaves,\n   - no other edges.\n\n2. Repeatedly, **connect two disconnected vertices of degree 1** (leaves) with an edge, until the graph becomes connected (i.e., a tree).\n\n3. The final graph $ T $ is a **tree with $ N $ vertices**, and we're to **recover the levels $ L_1, L_2, \\ldots, L_M $** of the initial stars.\n\n---\n\n### 🧠 Key Observations\n\n- Each **star of level $ k $** has:\n  - One **center** (degree $ k $),\n  - $ k $ **leaves** (degree 1).\n\n- During the process, we only add edges between **degree-1 vertices** that are **in different components**.\n\n- Important: **After connecting two leaves**, their degrees become 2, so they’re no longer eligible.\n\n- The key insight is that:\n\n> The number of **initial stars** is equal to the number of **nodes in the final tree whose degree is strictly greater than 2** **plus one**? Not exactly.\n\nWait — better idea comes from analyzing **how many leaves remain** and how many were **used in merging**.\n\nBut here's a known idea from similar problems:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Known Insight (From Problem Structure):\n\nLet’s define:\n- In the final tree $ T $, count the number of **vertices of degree 1** — these are leaves.\n- But not all leaves were \"original\" — some connections were added between original leaves.\n\nHowever, observe:\n\n> Each **original star of level $ k $** has exactly $ k $ **original leaves**. The center has degree $ k $ initially.\n\nBut during the merging process, **some of those leaves are connected to leaves from other stars**.\n\n- Each **edge added** in the merging process connects two leaves (degree 1 vertices).\n- Each such edge **uses up two leaves**.\n- The final tree has $ L $ **leaves** (i.e., vertices of degree 1).\n\nLet’s denote:\n- $ \\text{leaf\\_count} $: number of leaves in the final tree.\n- $ E_{\\text{added}} = (N - 1) - \\sum (\\text{initial edges}) $\n\nBut initially, total number of edges is $ \\sum_{i=1}^M (k_i) $, since each level-$ k_i $ star has $ k_i $ edges.\n\nWait — actually, each star of level $ k $ has $ k $ edges?  \nNo! A star with $ k+1 $ vertices and $ k $ edges → yes. So total initial edges = sum of levels.\n\nFinal tree has $ N - 1 $ edges.\n\nNumber of edges added = $ N - 1 - \\sum_{i=1}^{M} L_i $\n\nBut each added edge is between two leaves.\n\nLet’s think differently.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Correct Insight:\n\nLet’s say we started with $ M $ stars of levels $ L_1, L_2, \\ldots, L_M $.\n\nThen:\n- Total number of **vertices** = $ \\sum_{i=1}^M (L_i + 1) = N $\n  → $ \\sum L_i = N - M $\n\n- Total number of **initial leaves** = $ \\sum_{i=1}^M L_i = N - M $\n\n- Number of edges added during merging = $ N - 1 - \\sum L_i = N - 1 - (N - M) = M - 1 $\n\nEach added edge connects **2 leaves**, so total number of **leaves consumed** = $ 2(M - 1) $\n\nSo, number of **original leaves that remain as leaves** in the final tree is:\n$$\n(N - M) - 2(M - 1) = N - M - 2M + 2 = N - 3M + 2\n$$\n\nBut this must equal the number of leaves in the final tree!\n\nLet’s denote $ \\ell $ = number of leaves in the final tree.\n\nThen:\n$$\n\\ell = N - 3M + 2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad M = \\frac{N - \\ell + 2}{3}\n$$\n\nBut wait — is that always valid?\n\nLet’s test on Sample 1:\n\n- N = 6\n- Tree: 1-2-3-4-5-6 → path → leaves: 1 and 6 → $ \\ell = 2 $\n- Then $ M = (6 - 2 + 2)/3 = 6/3 = 2 $ → ✔️\n- Then $ \\sum L_i = N - M = 6 - 2 = 4 $\n- So levels: two stars with levels summing to 4 → possible (2,2)\n\n✔️ Output: 2 2\n\nSample 2:\n- N = 9\n- Count leaves.\n\nEdges:\n```\n3 9\n7 8\n8 6\n4 6\n4 1\n5 9\n7 3\n5 2\n```\n\nLet’s count degrees:\n- 1: [4] → deg 1\n- 2: [5] → deg 1\n- 3: [9,7] → deg 2\n- 4: [6,1] → deg 2\n- 5: [9,2] → deg 2\n- 6: [8,4] → deg 2\n- 7: [8,3] → deg 2\n- 8: [7,6] → deg 2\n- 9: [3,5] → deg 2\n\nWait — only 1 and 2 are leaves? deg(1)=1, deg(2)=1, others ≥2? But 9 is connected to 3 and 5 → deg 2. Then $ \\ell = 2 $\n\nThen $ M = (9 - 2 + 2)/3 = 9/3 = 3 $\n\nThen $ \\sum L_i = 9 - 3 = 6 $, so three stars, level sum 6 → average 2 → (2,2,2)\n\n✔️ Output: 2 2 2\n\nSample 3:\n- N = 20\n- Count leaves: vertices with degree 1.\n\nWe’ll process edges and count degrees.\n\nBut anyway, the formula seems solid.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ So the Plan:\n\n1. Read the tree.\n2. Compute degree of each vertex.\n3. Count number of leaves: $ \\ell = \\# \\{ v \\mid \\deg(v) = 1 \\} $\n4. Compute number of stars: $ M = \\frac{N - \\ell + 2}{3} $\n   → Should be integer.\n5. Then total level sum = $ N - M $\n6. But we need to **recover the individual levels**.\n\nHow?\n\nRecall:\n\n- Each **star's center** may have **final degree ≥ k**, but during the merging, we only added edges between leaves.\n- The **internal structure** of how centers' degrees evolve?\n\nWait — here's a **key observation**:\n\nWhen two **leaves are connected**, they are from **different components**. These **leaves were originally in stars**.\n\nBut when you connect a leaf from star A and leaf from star B, the edge is added, and both become degree 2.\n\nBut the **centers of the stars remain**, and their degrees in the final tree?\n\n- The center had initial degree = $ k $ (connected to $ k $ leaves).\n- Then, **some of those leaves may have been used in connections** to other components.\n\nBut the **center itself** never gets involved in new edges — it only has edges to its own leaves.\n\nHowever, those **leaves might have been connected to outside**, but that doesn't affect the degree of the center.\n\nBut wait — when a leaf is connected to another leaf, the leaf gains an extra edge, but the **center loses one connection**? No — the leaf is still connected to the center!\n\nYes! The edge from leaf to center **remains**. The leaf now has:\n- one edge to center,\n- one edge to the other leaf (from added edge).\n\nSo the center still has all its original edges to its leaves — **its degree remains $ k $**?  \nBut in the final tree, we may see **degree of center < k**?\n\nNo — unless some leaves were removed from its adjacency?\n\nBut no — the connections from the center to its leaves are never removed.\n\nAh! But the degree of the center **in the final tree** is still exactly $ k $, **unless** the center had some leaves that were connected to external — but that doesn’t change its own degree.\n\nSo: the **center's degree in the final tree is still the number of its leaves it started with**, i.e., $ k $.\n\nBut wait — suppose a leaf is connected to another component — it still has edge to center. So center degree unchanged.\n\nSo in the final tree, **each center appears as a vertex whose degree equals the level of the star**.\n\nBut what if a **center's leaf** is connected externally? The center still has degree $ k $.\n\nSo, **in the final tree**, the **degrees of the centers are equal to their original star levels**.\n\nBut can a center be indistinguishable from others?\n\nWait — not all high-degree vertices are centers.\n\nAlso: can a leaf become high degree? No — a leaf starts as degree 1, at most two edges added (but only one edge is added per vertex at most? no — any vertex can gain multiple edges? No, only two leaves are connected per operation, but a vertex can participate only once? Not necessarily.\n\nWait! A vertex can have **only one** chance to be degree 1? Once it gains an edge, its degree becomes 2, so it's never picked again.\n\nSo: **each vertex can be involved in at most one added edge**, and **only if it was a leaf**.\n\nTherefore:\n- The **original leaves** are the only ones that can gain an extra edge.\n- Each such leaf gains **0 or 1** extra edge.\n\nSo, in the final tree:\n- A vertex has degree = (original degree) + (1 if it was used in an added edge, else 0)\n\nNow:\n- The **center** had original degree = $ k \\geq 2 $, never changes — because only leaves (original degree 1) are selected.\n- So center's final degree = $ k \\geq 2 $\n- An **original leaf** had degree 1 initially:\n   - If it was **never used** in an added edge → final degree = 1\n   - If it was **used** in an added edge → final degree = 2\n\nSo:\n- In the final tree:\n   - All vertices with degree ≥ 3 must be **centers** (since leaves end up with 1 or 2)\n   - Vertices with degree 2 can be:\n       - a leaf that was used in an added edge, or\n       - a center of a level-2 star (which has degree 2)\n   - So degree 2 vertices could be either centers or used-up leaves.\n\nBut here's the key:\n\n- The **only vertices that were centers** are the ones that **originally had degree ≥ 2**, and their degree remains unchanged.\n\nBut since we only add edges incident to original leaves, the **center's degree is unchanged**.\n\nSo: **The list of star levels is exactly the list of degrees of the centers**.\n\nSo: if we could identify which vertices were **centers of initial stars**, then their degrees in the final tree are the $ L_i $.\n\nHow to identify which vertices were centers?\n\nLet’s think: centers never lose edges and are never involved in new edges → their **degree in final tree = their original degree = $ L_i \\geq 2 $**\n\nBut some vertices with degree 2 may be **used-up leaves** (originally degree 1, then connected once → degree 2), and are **not centers**.\n\nSo we need to **distinguish**:\n- degree-2 vertices that are **centers** (so were originally degree 2)\n- degree-2 vertices that are **used-up leaves** (originally degree 1)\n\nBut we don’t know.\n\nAlternative idea:\n\nUse the earlier formula:\n\nWe know:\n- $ M = \\frac{N - \\ell + 2}{3} $\n- $ \\ell = $ number of leaves in final tree (degree 1)\n- $ \\sum L_i = N - M $\n\nNow, suppose we look at all vertices with degree ≥ 2.\n\nBut more importantly:\n\nLet’s define:\n- $ C $: set of centers → $ |C| = M $\n- Then, the levels $ L_i $ are the degrees of these centers in the final tree.\n- But for **non-center vertices**:\n   - all are **original leaves**\n   - so their degrees are either 1 or 2 in the final tree\n\nTherefore:\n- any vertex with **degree ≥ 3** must be a **center**\n- any vertex with **degree 1** must be an **unused leaf** (so not a center)\n\n- any vertex with **degree 2** may be:\n   - a **center** of a level-2 star\n   - or a **used leaf** (original leaf that was paired)\n\nSo: centers are:\n- all vertices with degree ≥ 3\n- plus **some** of the degree-2 vertices\n\nLet $ x $ = number of centers that are level-2 → then they have degree 2 in final tree\n\nWe already know:\n- $ M = $ total number of centers\n- number of vertices with degree ≥ 3 = $ a $\n- then number of degree-2 vertices that are centers = $ M - a $\n\nLet $ d_2 $ = number of vertices with degree 2\n\nThen number of **used leaves** = number of degree-2 vertices that are **not centers** = $ d_2 - (M - a) $\n\nAlso:\n- total number of **original leaves** = $ N - M $\n- number of **unused original leaves** = $ \\ell $ = number of degree-1 vertices\n- number of **used original leaves** = $ (N - M) - \\ell $\n\nBut we also know that each added edge uses 2 leaves → total used leaves = $ 2(M - 1) $\n\nBecause $ M - 1 $ edges were added to connect $ M $ components into one.\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\text{used leaves} = 2(M - 1)\n$$\nand also:\n$$\n\\text{used leaves} = (N - M) - \\ell\n$$\n\nLet’s verify:\n- $ N - M = \\sum L_i $ = original leaves\n- subtract $ \\ell $ = leaves that remained as leaves → used = $ N - M - \\ell $\n- this should equal $ 2(M - 1) $\n\nCheck:\n$$\nN - M - \\ell = 2M - 2 \\Rightarrow N - \\ell = 3M - 2\n\\Rightarrow M = \\frac{N - \\ell + 2}{3}\n$$\nSame as before → consistent.\n\nBut now, back to reconstructing levels.\n\nIdea:\n\n- All centers are:\n   - vertices with deg ≥ 3 → must be centers\n   - and exactly $ M - a $ centers from deg=2 vertices\n\nBut which ones?\n\nBut note: the **level of a star is the degree of its center in the final tree**.\n\nBut if we can **find exactly $ M $ vertices** that were centers, then taking their degrees gives us the levels.\n\nWe know:\n- All deg ≥ 3 vertices are centers → $ a $ of them\n- We need $ M - a $ more centers → they must be among deg=2 vertices\n\nBut which ones?\n\nActually, we don’t care about identity — we only care about the **multiset of degrees of centers**.\n\nSo:\n- We need $ M - a $ centers of degree 2\n- And $ a $ centers of degrees ≥ 3\n\nSo the list of levels is:\n- all $ d[v] $ for $ v $ with $ d[v] \\geq 3 $\n- plus $ M - a $ copies of 2\n\nThen sort and output.\n\nLet’s test on samples.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Algorithm\n\n1. Read $ N $\n2. Build graph and compute degree of each vertex\n3. Count $ \\ell $ = number of degree-1 vertices\n4. Compute $ M = (N - \\ell + 2) // 3 $\n5. Count $ a $ = number of vertices with degree ≥ 3\n6. The levels are:\n   - for each vertex with degree ≥ 3: its degree\n   - plus $ M - a $ copies of 2\n7. Sort and print\n\nWait — does this work?\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Test Sample 1\n\nN = 6\nTree: path: 1-2-3-4-5-6\n\nDegrees:\n- 1: deg 1\n- 2: deg 2\n- 3: deg 2\n- 4: deg 2\n- 5: deg 2\n- 6: deg 1\n\n→ $ \\ell = 2 $\n→ $ M = (6 - 2 + 2) // 3 = 6 // 3 = 2 $\n\na = number of vertices with deg ≥ 3 → none → 0\n\nSo we need $ M - a = 2 $ centers of degree 2\n\nSo levels = [2,2] → sorted → \"2 2\" → ✔️\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Test Sample 2\n\nN=9\n\nDegrees:\n- From earlier:\n   - deg(1)=1\n   - deg(2)=1\n   - deg(3)=2\n   - deg(4)=2\n   - deg(5)=2\n   - deg(6)=2\n   - deg(7)=2\n   - deg(8)=2\n   - deg(9)=2\n\nSo $ \\ell = 2 $ → $ M = (9-2+2)/3 = 9/3 = 3 $\na = number with deg ≥ 3 → 0\nso add $ 3 - 0 = 3 $ copies of 2 → levels = [2,2,2] → ✔️\n\n---\n\n### 🧪 Test Sample 3\n\nWe need to process input:\n\n```\n20\n8 3\n8 18\n2 19\n8 20\n9 17\n19 7\n8 7\n14 12\n2 15\n14 10\n2 13\n2 16\n2 1\n9 5\n10 15\n14 6\n2 4\n2 11\n5 12\n9 5 -> d