    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed string word.\nIn one operation, you can pick any index i of word and change word[i] to any lowercase English letter.\nReturn the minimum number of operations needed to remove all adjacent almost-equal characters from word.\nTwo characters a and b are almost-equal if a == b or a and b are adjacent in the alphabet.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"aaaaa\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: We can change word into \"acaca\" which does not have any adjacent almost-equal characters.\nIt can be shown that the minimum number of operations needed to remove all adjacent almost-equal characters from word is 2.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"abddez\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: We can change word into \"ybdoez\" which does not have any adjacent almost-equal characters.\nIt can be shown that the minimum number of operations needed to remove all adjacent almost-equal characters from word is 2.\nExample 3:\n\nInput: word = \"zyxyxyz\"\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can change word into \"zaxaxaz\" which does not have any adjacent almost-equal characters. \nIt can be shown that the minimum number of operations needed to remove all adjacent almost-equal characters from word is 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= word.length <= 100\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step-by-step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We need to remove all adjacent \"almost-equal\" characters from a string\n2. Two characters are almost-equal if:\n   - They are the same character (a == b), OR\n   - They are adjacent in the alphabet (like 'a' and 'b', 'm' and 'n')\n3. We can change any character to any lowercase English letter\n4. We want to minimize the number of operations\n\n## Analysis\n\nLet me think about the approach:\n- We need to scan through the string and identify pairs of adjacent almost-equal characters\n- When we find such a pair, we need to break it by changing one of the characters\n- The key insight is that we want to be greedy - when we change a character, we should choose a letter that won't create new almost-equal pairs with neighbors\n\n## Strategy\n\n1. Iterate through the string from left to right\n2. For each position, check if it forms an almost-equal pair with the previous character\n3. If yes, we need to change the current character (greedy approach - changing the current one doesn't affect already processed pairs)\n4. When changing, choose a character that is not almost-equal to either the previous or next character\n\n## Step-by-step Solution\n\n1. First, create a helper function to check if two characters are almost-equal\n2. Use a greedy approach: if word[i] and word[i-1] are almost-equal, change word[i]\n3. When changing, pick a character that differs by at least 2 from both word[i-1] and word[i+1] (if exists)\n\n```python\ndef removeAlmostEqualCharacters(word):\n    def are_almost_equal(a, b):\n        return a == b or abs(ord(a) - ord(b)) == 1\n    \n    # Convert string to list for easier manipulation\n    chars = list(word)\n    operations = 0\n    \n    # Start from index 1 and check with previous character\n    for i in range(1, len(chars)):\n        if are_almost_equal(chars[i], chars[i-1]):\n            # Need to change chars[i]\n            operations += 1\n            \n            # Choose a character that is not almost-equal to chars[i-1]\n            # and preferably not almost-equal to chars[i+1] if it exists\n            for c in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':\n                if not are_almost_equal(c, chars[i-1]):\n                    # Check if this would create problem with next character\n                    if i + 1 < len(chars) and are_almost_equal(c, chars[i+1]):\n                        continue\n                    chars[i] = c\n                    break\n    \n    return operations\n```",
