    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_320",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi participated in N contests and earned a performance P_i in the i-th contest.\nHe wants to choose some (at least one) contests from these and maximize his rating calculated from the results of those contests.\nFind the maximum possible rating he can achieve by optimally choosing the contests.\nHere, Takahashi's rating R is calculated as the following, where k is the number of chosen contests and (Q_1, Q_2, \\ldots, Q_k) are the performances in the chosen contests in the order he participated:\n\n\\displaystyle R=\\frac{{\\sum_{{i=1}}^k (0.9)^{{k-i}}Q_i}}{{\\sum_{{i=1}}^k (0.9)^{{k-i}}-\\frac{{1200}}{{\\sqrt{{k}}.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nP_1 P_2 \\ldots P_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the maximum possible rating that Takahashi can achieve.\nYour output will be considered correct if the absolute or relative error from the true value is at most 10^{{-6}}.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 5000\n- 1\\leq P_i\\leq 5000\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n1000 600 1200\n\nSample Output 1\n\n256.735020470879931\n\nIf Takahashi chooses the first and third contests, his rating will be:\n\\displaystyle R=\\frac{{0.9\\times 1000+ 1.0\\times 1200}}{{0.9+1.0}}-\\frac{{1200}}{{\\sqrt{{2}}=256.73502....\nThis is the maximum possible rating.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\n600 1000 1200\n\nSample Output 2\n\n261.423219407873376\n\nThe rating is maximized when all the first, second, and third contests are selected.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n1\n100\n\nSample Output 3\n\n-1100.000000000000000\n\nThe rating can also be negative.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's carefully analyze the problem.\n\nWe are given:\n- Takahashi participated in `N` contests.\n- In each contest `i`, he got performance `P_i`.\n- He can choose **at least one** subset of contests.\n- The **rating** `R` is computed as:\n\n$$\nR = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{k} (0.9)^{k-i} Q_i}{\\sum_{i=1}^{k} (0.9)^{k-i}} - \\frac{1200}{\\sqrt{k}}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- `k` is the number of chosen contests,\n- $ Q_1, Q_2, \\ldots, Q_k $ are the performances in **the order they were taken** (i.e., the order from the original list).\n\nSo importantly:\n- The **weights decrease exponentially backward** — the **last contest has weight 1.0**, second-to-last has weight 0.9, third-to-last 0.81, etc.\n- But the numerator and denominator both use weights $ w_j = 0.9^{k - i} $ for the `i-th` chosen contest (in participation order).\n\nSo if we choose a subsequence of contests (preserving order), we compute:\n\n$$\nR = \\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{k} 0.9^{k-j} Q_j}{\\sum_{j=1}^{k} 0.9^{k-j}} - \\frac{1200}{\\sqrt{k}}\n$$\n\nNote: The denominator is a geometric sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^k (0.9)^{k-j} = \\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} (0.9)^i = \\frac{1 - 0.9^k}{1 - 0.9} = 10 (1 - 0.9^k)\n$$\n\nBut actually wait: denominator is sum of weights:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} 0.9^i = \\frac{1 - 0.9^k}{1 - 0.9} = 10 \\cdot (1 - 0.9^k)\n$$\n\nBut no! The formula in the problem is written:\n\n> $$\nR = \\frac{\\sum (0.9)^{k-i} Q_i}{\\sum (0.9)^{k-i}} - \\frac{1200}{\\sqrt{k}}\n$$\n\nSo the **denominator is the sum of the weights**, not part of a normalization term subtracted incorrectly.\n\nSo rating is:\n\n$$\nR = \\left( \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^k w_i Q_i}{\\sum_{i=1}^k w_i} \\right) - \\frac{1200}{\\sqrt{k}}, \\quad \\text{where } w_i = (0.9)^{k-i}\n$$\n\nThat is: it's a **weighted average of the selected performances**, with more recent (later in order) ones weighted more heavily, minus a penalty term $ 1200 / \\sqrt{k} $.\n\nOur goal: choose any **non-empty subsequence** (not necessarily contiguous) of the contests, **preserving the order**, to **maximize this rating**.\n\nConstraints: $ N \\leq 5000 $\n\nSo we can't try all $ 2^N $ subsets.\n\nBut note: We are allowed to **choose any subsequence** (order preserved), so we need to decide for each contest: pick or not?\n\nIdea:\nWe can use **dynamic programming**. But the formula depends on:\n- The **number of contests selected so far (k)** — for penalty and for weighting\n- And the **last index picked** — because the weight depends on **position in the selected sequence**, not in the original\n\nBut the weights depend on the **relative position in the chosen subsequence**: the last chosen contest gets weight 1.0, the one before 0.9, etc.\n\nSo if we define DP where:\n- `dp[i][k]` = maximum possible numerator (weighted sum) of a subsequence ending at or before `i`, with `k` elements, and also track denominator sum?\n\nBut we are interested in the **average minus penalty**, which is nonlinear. So maximizing numerator doesn't directly help.\n\nAlternate idea:\nTry **all possible values of k** (lengths from 1 to N), and for each k, find the **maximum weighted average** (with weights decreasing from left to right, i.e., last element weight 1.0, first element weight $0.9^{k-1}$) that we can achieve from some increasing index sequence of length k.\n\nBut the weights depend on **position in the subsequence**, not in the original array.\n\nSo if we fix the length `k`, and we pick indices: $ i_1 < i_2 < \\ldots < i_k $, then:\n- The performance at $i_1$ gets weight $0.9^{k-1}$\n- At $i_2$ gets weight $0.9^{k-2}$\n- ...\n- At $i_k$ gets weight $0.9^0 = 1.0$\n\nSo the weights increase exponentially toward the end of the subsequence.\n\nSo for fixed `k`, we want to maximize:\n\n$$\n\\text{weighted sum} = \\sum_{j=1}^k (0.9)^{k-j} P_{i_j}\n$$\n\nAnd the denominator is:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^k (0.9)^{k-j} = \\frac{1 - 0.9^k}{1 - 0.9} = 10 \\cdot (1 - 0.9^k)\n$$\n\nWait, no: $ \\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} 0.9^j = \\frac{1 - 0.9^k}{0.1} = 10(1 - 0.9^k) $\n\nYes.\n\nSo for a fixed subsequence of length `k`, the **weighted average** is:\n\n$$\n\\text{avg}_k = \\frac{\\text{weighted sum}}{10(1 - 0.9^k)}\n$$\n\nAnd rating:\n\n$$\nR = \\text{avg}_k - \\frac{1200}{\\sqrt{k}}\n$$\n\nSo to maximize $R$, for each `k`, we can:\n1. Compute the best possible **weighted sum** of any increasing subsequence of length `k`, where the weights are $0.9^{k-1}, 0.9^{k-2}, \\ldots, 1.0$ (assigned in order of selection).\n2. Then compute corresponding $R$\n3. Take max over $k = 1$ to $N$\n\nSo we can do:\n- Precompute for each `k` (from 1 to N), the maximum weighted sum possible for any subsequence of length `k`.\n- Then compute rating.\n\nDP idea for fixed `k`:\nLet’s define:\n- `dp[j][m]` = maximum weighted sum achievable using the first `j` contests and selecting `m` of them.\n\nBut the weights depend on **when** the element was selected in the subsequence: earlier selected = smaller weight.\n\nBut the weights are fixed for positions: the first selected gets weight $0.9^{k-1}$, second $0.9^{k-2}$, ..., last $1.0$.\n\nBut wait: for a fixed `k`, if we don't know when we're going to end, we can't assign weights.\n\nBut if we **fix k**, we can run a DP that builds sequences of length up to `k`, and when we assign a position in the sequence, we multiply by the appropriate weight.\n\nBut key observation: the weights are increasing toward the end. So the **last element selected gets the largest weight**.\n\nImportant: We can reverse the array and reverse the weights?\n\nIdea:\nReverse the contest list: from last to first.\nThen choose a subsequence of length `k`, now in reversed order.\nThen the **first in this reversed order** is the **last in original**, so gets weight 1.0.\nThe next gets 0.9, etc.\n\nSo weights are decreasing.\n\nSo for fixed `k`, to compute maximum weighted sum with weights $ [w_0=1.0, w_1=0.9, w_2=0.81, \\dots, w_{k-1}=0.9^{k-1}] $, we can:\n\n- Reverse the array: `P_rev = P[::-1]`\n- Use DP where `dp[i][j