      "generated_answer": "We need to check if T can be formed from S by either:\n\n1. Taking 3 letters from S (as a subsequence, uppercase them) to match T exactly.\n2. Taking 2 letters from S (as a subsequence, uppercase them) and appending 'X' to match T.\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n- If T[2] == 'X', we can try matching T[0] and T[1] as a subsequence in S, ignoring the 'X'.\n- Otherwise, we must match all three characters of T as a subsequence in S.\n\nWe can solve this by scanning S sequentially to find the required letters in order.\n\n---\n\nHere’s the Python implementation:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    S = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    T = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n\n    # Method 2: last char is X, match first two chars\n    if T[2] == 'X':\n        # Try to find T[0] and T[1] as subsequence in S\n        i = 0\n        for ch in S:\n            if i < 2 and ch.upper() == T[i]:\n                i += 1\n        if i == 2:\n            print(\"Yes\")\n            return\n\n    # Method 1: match all three chars as subsequence\n    i = 0\n    for ch in S:\n        if i < 3 and ch.upper() == T[i]:\n            i += 1\n    if i == 3:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
