      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    # Read the distance matrix in upper triangular form\n    idx = 1\n    D = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    \n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n + 1):\n            D[i][j] = int(data[idx])\n            D[j][i] = int(data[idx])\n            idx += 1\n    \n    # Use bitmask DP where dp[mask] represents the maximum total weight \n    # for matching nodes indicated by mask (1-indexed)\n    # We use bit 0 for node 1, bit 1 for node 2, ..., bit n-1 for node n\n    \n    dp = [-10**18] * (1 << n)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    # Iterate over all masks\n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        if dp[mask] < 0:\n            continue\n        \n        # Find the first unpaired vertex\n        u = -1\n        for i in range(n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << i)):\n                u = i\n                break\n        \n        if u == -1:\n            continue\n        \n        # Try pairing u with v > u\n        for v in range(u + 1, n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << v)):\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << u) | (1 << v)\n                w = D[u + 1][v + 1]\n                if dp[new_mask] < dp[mask] + w:\n                    dp[new_mask] = dp[mask] + w\n    \n    # The answer is dp[(1 << n) - 1], but note: if n is odd, we can't match all vertices\n    # So we want max over all masks with even number of bits set?\n    # Actually, the problem says \"endpoints of chosen edges are pairwise distinct\", \n    # so it's a matching — we want the maximum-weight matching (not necessarily perfect).\n    # But our DP computes max for every subset; we should take the max over all masks.\n    \n    ans = 0\n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        if dp[mask] > ans:\n            ans = dp[mask]\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
