      "generated_answer": "Let’s reason through this carefully.\n\nWe know there was originally a solid rectangular block of cookies `#` in the grid (height ≥ 2, width ≥ 2).  \nOne cookie was removed from that rectangle, leaving one `.` inside it.\n\nThus, in the given input:\n- All `#` squares are either part of the original rectangle or outside of it (but actually, outside there are no cookies originally, so `.` outside is fine).\n- The rectangle’s boundary in the input will look almost all `#`, except at most one `.` inside it — that’s the eaten cookie.\n\nSo we can find the rectangle’s bounds by:\n1. Finding the min and max row indices where `#` appears.\n2. Finding the min and max col indices where `#` appears.\n3. Within that rectangle, finding the only `.` (if any), which is the eaten cookie.\n\nBut wait — in the sample 3, rectangle bounds min row = 1, max row = 4, min col = 3, max col = 6? Let’s check.\n\nActually, simpler:  \nThe original rectangle is contiguous in both dimensions.  \nSo after removal, every row in the rectangle has the same number of `#` except maybe one row (where the cookie was removed), but careful — the cookie could be anywhere inside rectangle, so maybe all rows still have same number of `#` except one row? No, because removing one cookie changes counts per row and per column.\n\nBetter approach:  \nLet’s find the top-left `(a, c)` and bottom-right `(b, d)` of the original cookie rectangle from the current `#` pattern.  \nSince only one cookie missing, the rectangle bounds are still the min/max of rows/cols containing any `#`.\n\nSo:\n- `top = min row index (1-based) where there is any '#',`\n- `bottom = max row index where there is any '#',`\n- `left = min col index where there is any '#',`\n- `right = max col index where there is any '#'.`\n\nInside the rectangle `[top, bottom] × [left, right]`, every cell should be `#` except one cell which is `.`. Find that cell.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Read H, W and the grid.\n2. Initialize top = H+1, bottom = -1, left = W+1, right = -1.\n3. Loop over grid: if `#` at `(i, j)`, update top = min(top, i), bottom = max(bottom, i), left = min(left, j), right = max(right, j).\n4. Then loop over i from top to bottom, j from left to right: if grid[i][j] == '.', print i, j.\n\nThis works because the missing cookie is inside that bounding box.\n\nLet’s implement:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not input:\n        return\n    H = int(input[0])\n    W = int(input[1])\n    grid = input[2:]\n\n    top = H + 1\n    bottom = -1\n    left = W + 1\n    right = -1\n\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                top = min(top, i + 1)  # 1-based row\n                bottom = max(bottom, i + 1)\n                left = min(left, j + 1)  # 1-based col\n                right = max(right, j + 1)\n\n    for i in range(top, bottom + 1):\n        for j in range(left, right + 1):\n            if grid[i - 1][j - 1] == '.':\n                print(i, j)\n                return\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
