      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    # Parse first line\n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    S = data[1].strip()\n    T = data[2].strip()\n    \n    # X starts as all '#' characters\n    X = ['#'] * N\n    \n    # We need to check if we can transform X to S\n    # We can only replace M consecutive characters with T\n    \n    # Create the target string as list for comparison\n    target = list(S)\n    \n    # We'll use dynamic programming / greedy approach\n    # Since M is at most 5, we can use a simpler approach\n    \n    # Check each position where we could potentially place T\n    # We need to verify if it's possible to achieve S\n    \n    # Work from left to right, but we need to be careful about overlapping\n    # Instead, let's think about this differently:\n    # For each position i in S, if S[i] != T[i-pos] for some placement of T starting at pos,\n    # then we need to make sure this character can be set by some T placement\n    \n    # Actually, let's use a different approach:\n    # We'll simulate the reverse process - start from S and see if we can reduce it\n    # But that might be complex. Let's try a DP approach.\n    \n    # Since M is small (≤ 5), we can use state-based DP\n    # For each position, we track if it's possible to have S[0:i] matchable\n    \n    # Actually, simpler approach:\n    # For each position i from 0 to N-M, check if we can place T starting at i\n    # But we need to make sure all characters in S can be covered\n    \n    # Let's use a different insight:\n    # A character S[i] must either be:\n    # 1. Covered by some T placement that sets it correctly, or\n    # 2. Never changed (but initially everything is '#', so it must be changed)\n    \n    # So every position must be covered by at least one valid T placement\n    \n    # We can use DP where dp[i] = True if we can match S[0:i] properly\n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True  # Empty prefix is always possible\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if not dp[i]:\n            continue\n            \n        # From position i, we can try to place T starting at position i\n        # This would cover positions [i, i+M-1]\n        if i + M <= N:\n            # Check if placing T at position i is compatible with S\n            valid = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[i + j] != T[j]:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if valid:\n                dp[i + M] = True\n        \n        # Also, we can skip position i (if already matched by previous operations)\n        # Actually, we need to consider that positions can be set by overlapping operations\n        # But our DP state means we've matched up to position i\n        \n        # We also need to handle the case where position i is already set and we don't start T here\n        # So we should also propagate the dp state forward by 1\n        if i + 1 <= N:\n            dp[i + 1] = dp[i + 1] or dp[i]\n    \n    # However, the above DP doesn't fully capture the problem.\n    # Let's think more carefully:\n    \n    # Actually, let's use a simpler greedy approach with the constraint that M is very small (≤ 5)\n    # We can use a BFS/DFS approach, but N can be up to 200,000\n    \n    # Alternative insight:\n    # We can place T at various positions. The question is whether we can cover S completely.\n    # A character at position i can be set by any placement of T that covers position i.\n    # Specifically, T can be placed starting at position j where j ≤ i ≤ j+M-1, i.e., i-M+1 ≤ j ≤ i.\n    \n    # For each position i in S, there must exist some starting position j in [max(0,i-M+1), min(i, N-M+1)]\n    # such that when we place T at j, T[i-j] == S[i]\n    \n    # But this isn't sufficient because placements affect multiple positions simultaneously.\n    \n    # Let's try a different DP:\n    # dp[i] = True if we can properly match S[0:i]\n    # To compute dp[i], we consider that position i-1 must be covered by some T placement ending at i, i+1, ..., i+M-1\n    # i.e., T placement starts at i-M, i-M+1, ..., i-1\n    \n    # Actually, let's reset and use this approach:\n    # We'll use dp[i] = True if prefix S[0:i] can be matched\n    # dp[0] = True\n    # For each position i, dp[i] is true if either:\n    # - dp[i-1] is true (we don't start T here), OR\n    # - There exists k such that i-M == k and dp[k] is true and T matches S[k:i]\n    \n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        # Option 1: don't place T ending at position i\n        if i > 0:\n            dp[i] = dp[i-1]\n        \n        # Option 2: place T ending at position i (i.e., starting at i-M)\n        if i >= M:\n            start_pos = i - M\n            # Check if T matches S[start_pos:i]\n            valid = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[start_pos + j] != T[j]:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if valid and dp[start_pos]:\n                dp[i] = True\n    \n    # But this still isn't right because a position can be overwritten multiple times.\n    # The key insight is that we can apply operations in any order, and later operations\n    # can overwrite earlier ones. So we need to check if there's a sequence of operations\n    # that results in S.\n    \n    # Let's use a different approach: greedy from left to right.\n    # We'll simulate the process of building S from X.\n    # We can only place T at various positions.\n    \n    # Since M is small, we can use a DP that tracks the state of the last M-1 characters.\n    # But let's try a simpler method:\n    \n    # We can use the following observation:\n    # For the transformation to be possible, every position i in S must be \"achievable\",\n    # meaning there must be some placement of T that can set position i to S[i].\n    # However, placements overlap, so we need to be more careful.\n    \n    # Actually, here's a correct approach:\n    # Use dynamic programming where dp[i] = True if we can match S[0:i]\n    # To extend dp[i], we consider placing T starting at position i\n    # But also, positions can be set by T placements that started earlier\n    \n    # Let me re-read the problem. We start with X = \"####...#\" (N times)\n    # We can replace any M consecutive characters with T.\n    # We want to know if we can make X equal S.\n    \n    # Key insight: since we can perform operations any number of times, and later operations\n    # can overwrite earlier ones, we only care about the final state.\n    \n    # A character at position i in the final string must be set by some operation that covers position i.\n    # Specifically, if we place T starting at position j, it affects positions j to j+M-1.\n    # For position i to be correct, there must be some j in [i-M+1, i] such that:\n    # - 0 <= j <= N-M (valid starting position)\n    # - T[i-j] == S[i]\n    \n    # But this is necessary but not sufficient, because we need consistent placements.\n    \n    # Let's use a greedy algorithm from left to right:\n    # We'll keep track of what positions have been definitively set.\n    # However, due to overwrites, we need to be strategic.\n    \n    # Actually, let's use this approach:\n    # We can solve this with DP where the state is our progress through the string.\n    # dp[i] = True if we can match S[0:i] with some sequence of operations.\n    # To compute dp[i], we consider:\n    # - We could have reached i without placing a T ending at i\n    # - Or we could place a T that ends at i (starts at i-M)\n    \n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if not dp[i]:\n            continue\n            \n        # We can always extend by not placing T starting at i\n        dp[i + 1] = True\n        \n        # Try placing T starting at position i\n        if i + M <= N:\n            # Check if placing T at position i is compatible with S[i:i+M]\n            compatible = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[i + j] != T[j]:\n                    compatible = False\n                    break\n            \n            if compatible:\n                # If we place T at position i, it will set positions i to i+M-1\n                # This doesn't necessarily mean dp[i+M] becomes true immediately,\n                # but we'll let the DP propagate\n                pass  # We'll handle this differently\n    \n    # Let me try a different perspective inspired by string covering:\n    # We want to cover the string with intervals [i, i+M-1] where T matches S[i:i+M]\n    # And we want to know if we can cover all positions 0 to N-1.\n    \n    # Use DP: dp[i] = True if we can cover positions 0 to i-1\n    dp = [False] * (N + 1)\n    dp[0] = True\n    \n    for i in range(N):\n        if not dp[i]:\n            continue\n            \n        # From position i, we can choose to place T starting at i\n        if i + M <= N:\n            # Check if T matches S[i:i+M]\n            valid = True\n            for j in range(M):\n                if S[i + j] != T[j]:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            \n            if valid:\n                dp[i + M] = True\n        \n        # We can also skip position i (move forward by 1)\n        dp[i + 1] = dp[i + 1] or dp[i]\n    \n    if dp[N]:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
