      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef min_cost_to_equalize(nums, cost1, cost2):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    if len(nums) == 1:\n        return 0\n    \n    max_val = max(nums)\n    total_ops = sum(max_val - x for x in nums)\n    \n    # If cost2 >= 2*cost1, always use cost1 operations\n    if cost2 >= 2 * cost1:\n        return (total_ops * cost1) % MOD\n    \n    # Otherwise, try all possible target values from max_val down to max_val - len(nums)\n    # Since increasing beyond max_val is never optimal for minimizing cost\n    min_cost = float('inf')\n    \n    for target in range(max_val, max(0, max_val - len(nums) - 1), -1):\n        diff = [target - x for x in nums]\n        if any(d < 0 for d in diff):\n            continue\n            \n        total_diff = sum(diff)\n        single_ops = total_diff  # Maximum possible single ops needed\n        # Each double op covers 2 increments, so max double ops = floor(total_diff / 2)\n        # But limited by count of non-zero elements\n        \n        # Count how many elements need to be increased\n        needs_increase = sum(1 for d in diff if d > 0)\n        \n        # Max double ops we can do is min(total_diff // 2, needs_increase)\n        max_double_ops = min(total_diff // 2, needs_increase)\n        \n        # Use as many double ops as possible: each double op saves (2*cost1 - cost2)\n        double_ops = max_double_ops\n        remaining_single = total_diff - 2 * double_ops\n        \n        cost = double_ops * cost2 + remaining_single * cost1\n        min_cost = min(min_cost, cost)\n    \n    return min_cost % MOD\n```",
