      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it)); L = int(next(it))\n    \n    a = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(N)]\n    b = [int(next(it)) for _ in range(M)]\n    \n    # Convert to 0-indexed\n    # Store forbidden pairs in a set of tuples (main_idx, side_idx)\n    forbidden = set()\n    for _ in range(L):\n        ci = int(next(it)) - 1\n        di = int(next(it)) - 1\n        forbidden.add((ci, di))\n    \n    # For each main dish, we want to find the most expensive side dish such that (i, j) is not forbidden\n    # Preprocess: sort side dishes with indices by price descending\n    side_sorted = sorted(range(M), key=lambda j: b[j], reverse=True)\n    \n    max_price = 0\n    \n    # For each main dish i, iterate through top side dishes until we find a valid one\n    for i in range(N):\n        for j_idx in range(min(len(side_sorted), 2)):  # Optimization: only check top few sides\n            j = side_sorted[j_idx]\n            if (i, j) not in forbidden:\n                price = a[i] + b[j]\n                if price > max_price:\n                    max_price = price\n                break\n    \n    # But if no side dish works for a main dish, we need to look further\n    # We might miss cases where top sides are forbidden but later ones work\n    # Alternative approach: consider top candidates from both arrays\n    \n    # Better approach: create list of (price, idx) for main and side\n    # Take top (L+1) from both since at most L combinations are forbidden\n    \n    # Find top min(L+1, N) main dishes\n    top_mains = sorted(range(N), key=lambda i: a[i], reverse=True)[:L+1]\n    # Find top min(L+1, M) side dishes\n    top_sides = sorted(range(M), key=lambda j: b[j], reverse=True)[:L+1]\n    \n    max_val = 0\n    for i in top_mains:\n        for j in top_sides:\n            if (i, j) not in forbidden:\n                val = a[i] + b[