      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    import heapq\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    k = int(data[1])\n    q = int(data[2])\n    \n    A = [0] * n\n    # Use a min-heap to maintain the largest K elements\n    heap = []\n    total = 0\n    \n    index = 3\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(q):\n        x = int(data[index]) - 1  # Convert to 0-indexed\n        y = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        \n        old_val = A[x]\n        A[x] = y\n        \n        # If old value was in the top K, remove it from sum and heap tracking\n        if len(heap) > 0 and heap[0] < old_val:\n            # Cannot directly remove, so we'll use lazy deletion concept\n            pass\n        \n        # Add new value\n        if len(heap) < k:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, y)\n            total += y\n        elif y > heap[0]:\n            removed = heapq.heapreplace(heap, y)\n            total += y - removed\n        else:\n            # New value not in top K, but check if old value was in heap\n            if old_val >= heap[0]:\n                # Old value was in the top K, so we need to adjust\n                total -= old_val\n                # Push new value only if it qualifies\n                if y >= heap[0]:\n                    heapq.heappushpop(heap, y)\n                    total += y\n                else:\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, y)\n                    total += y\n                    # Remove smallest now to keep size k\n                    extra = heapq.heappop(heap)\n                    total -= extra\n            # Otherwise, no change to the top K\n        \n        # Recompute total from scratch every time for correctness (slower but safe)\n        # Instead: simply sort and take top K\n        sorted_vals = sorted(A, reverse=True)\n        total = sum(sorted_vals[:k])\n        \n        results.append(str(total))\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nmain()\n```",
