  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_640",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose Q is a measure of output, K is a measure of capital stock in use, and L is a measure of the size of the employed labor force. Q =AK^\\betaL^\\gamma is a much used form of production function. When \\beta + \\gamma = 1, it is known as a Cobb-Douglas production function. This function has many convenient properties: Marginal products of both labor and capital are positive if \\beta and \\gamma are. If \\beta + \\gamma = 1, the function has constant returns to scale. That is, if both inputs are increased in like proportion, output will increase in that same proportion. A doubling of both inputs, for example, will result in doubled output. Discuss how the concept of linear regression might be applied to estimate \\beta and \\gamma .\n\nOptions: \nA. Polynomial regression\nB. Linear progression\nC. Quantile regression\nD. Ridge regression\nE. Logistic regression\nF. Exponential regression\nG. Lasso regression\nH. Multiple regression\nI. Single regression\nJ. Non-linear regression\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Cobb-Douglas production function $ Q = AK^\\beta L^\\gamma $ is nonlinear in parameters, but taking natural logs yields $ \\ln Q = \\ln A + \\beta \\ln K + \\gamma \\ln L $, which is linear in logged variables and parameters. This transformed equation can be estimated using ordinary least squares, a form of linear regression—specifically, **multiple regression** since it includes two predictors ($ \\ln K $ and $ \\ln L $). Although the original model is nonlinear, the log-linearized version fits standard multiple linear regression.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{H}\n$$",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 320,
        "output_tokens": 126,
        "total_tokens": 446
      },
      "provider": "local-vllm",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.00019009999999999999,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
