    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_590",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mr. Fields is considering selling a property acquired 15 years ago for $65,000; $10,000 was for the land, and $55,000 was for the building. Mr. Fields can now sell the property for $120,000. He can also keep it and continue to collect the annual rent from the building. If Mr. Fields decides to keep the property, he will keep it for another 25 years and will, at that time, sell it for $40,000. The annual rent receipts will be $5,930 for the next twenty-five years. Should Mr. Fields sell the property now or in twenty-five years? Assume that the building, but not the land, has been depreciated using the straight line method at 2%, long term gains are taxable at a rate of 25%, Mr. Fields' income tax rate is 56%, the minimum attractive rate of return after taxes is 2(1/2)%, and taxes will hold for the next 25 years. Make all calculations to the nearest dollar.\n\nOptions: \nA. Mr. Fields should lease the property to a new tenant for a higher annual rent\nB. Mr. Fields should sell the property now for $120,000\nC. Mr. Fields should keep the property for 25 more years\nD. Mr. Fields should convert the building into a personal residence\nE. Mr. Fields should rent the property for another 15 years\nF. Mr. Fields should sell the property after 10 years\nG. Mr. Fields should renovate the property and then decide whether to sell or keep it\nH. Mr. Fields should exchange the property for a similar one to defer taxes\nI. Mr. Fields should hold the property indefinitely for future generations\nJ. Mr. Fields should donate the property to a charitable organization for a tax write-off\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the best option, we must compare the after-tax net present value (NPV) of selling now versus holding for 25 years. Selling now yields $120,000; the building’s depreciation is $55,000 × 2% × 15 = $16,500, leaving a tax basis in the building of $38,500. The gain on the building is $120,000 - $10,000 (land) - $38,500 = $71,500, with long-term capital gains tax at 25% on $71,500 = $17,875; after-tax proceeds are $102,125. Holding the property yields 25 years of after-tax rent: $5,930 × (1 − 0.56) = $2,609.20 annually, plus $40,000 sale proceeds in 25 years. Discounting all cash flows at 2.5%, the NPV of holding is less than $102,125.  \n\nThus, selling now provides higher after-tax value.  \n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 474,
        "output_tokens": 273,
        "total_tokens": 747
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
