    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Without the preceding question, we can only infer based on economic principles.  \nIn standard microeconomics, the profit-maximizing hiring rule for labor is to hire until the wage equals the marginal revenue product (MRP).  \nTherefore, if wage > MRP, you should hire less labor; if wage < MRP, you could profit by hiring more, so optimal hiring occurs when wage = MRP.\n\nGiven the options, the correct basis for that condition is described in choice **A**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{A}",
