    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The production possibility frontier is a straight line when the opportunity cost is constant, which occurs when resources are not specialized or are equally productive between the two goods.  \n\nFrom the options given, **D** and **I** both describe conditions for a linear PPF, but **I** (all resources are equally productive) is essentially another way of saying resources are not specialized, and it matches constant productivity across goods. However, in many standard economics texts, the straight-line PPF is explicitly linked to **resources not being specialized** (D) or **perfect substitutes in production**, though **J** refers to perfect substitutes *in consumption*, which does not affect the PPF shape.  \n\nThe most precise answer given typical multiple-choice expectations is **D** (resources are not specialized).  \n\n\\boxed{D}",
