      "generated_answer": "The taxpayer plaintiff in a federal case has standing to challenge a congressional appropriation only if the tax and spending measure violates a specific constitutional limitation on the taxing and spending power. Under Flast v. Cohen, such standing exists when the taxpayer challenges spending under the Taxing and Spending Clause as violating a specific constitutional prohibition (e.g., Establishment Clause). Here, the plaintiff must show a nexus between the tax and the allegedly unconstitutional expenditure; only the purchaser of marijuana cigarettes (Option I) pays the challenged tax and can assert a direct financial injury from its use for a potentially non-public purpose.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
