      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 \\mid 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $.  \nLet $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substitute into the expression:  \n$ 3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $.  \nSo we require $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ d \\mid d \\cdot (\\text{anything}) $, we analyze divisibility:  \n$ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. As $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, $ d $ must divide $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $.  \nNow compute $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\mod d $:  \n$ d^2 \\equiv 0 $, $ -4d \\equiv 0 $, so $ 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\equiv 3 \\cdot 13 = 39 \\pmod{d} $.  \nThus, $ d \\mid 39 $. The positive divisors of 39 are $ 1, 3, 13, 39 $.  \nFor each $ d $, compute $ n = d - 2 $:  \n- $ d = 1 \\Rightarrow n = -1 $ (not positive)  \n- $ d = 3 \\Rightarrow n = 1 $  \n- $ d = 13 \\Rightarrow n = 11 $  \n- $ d = 39 \\Rightarrow n = 37 $  \nCheck if these satisfy original condition:  \n- $ n = 1 $: $ n + 2 = 3 $, product $ 3(4)(1 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 $, $ 3 \\mid 120 $ ✓  \n- $ n = 11 $: $ 13 \\mid 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 130 = 5460 $, $ 5460 / 13 = 420 $ ✓  \n- $ n = 37 $: $ 39 \\mid 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3 \\cdot 40 \\cdot 1378 = 165360 $, $ 165360 / 39 = 4240 $ ✓  \nSum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.  \n\n\\boxed{{49}}",
