      "generated_answer": "The composition $ f(g(x)) $ has degree $ 6 \\cdot 3 = 18 $. For $ h(f(x)) $, if $ h $ has degree $ d $, then $ h(f(x)) $ has degree $ d \\cdot 6 $. The highest degree term in the sum must be 36, so $ 6d = 36 $ implies $ d = 6 $. However, $ g(h(x)) $ has degree $ 3d $, which is maximized when $ d = 9 $ giving $ 3 \\cdot 9 = 27 $, still less than 36. Only $ h(f(x)) $ can reach degree 36 if $ \\deg h = 6 $. But checking consistency, to get total degree 36, the dominant term must come from $ h(f(x)) $, requiring $ \\deg h \\cdot 6 = 36 \\Rightarrow \\deg h = 6 $. \n\n\\boxed{C}",
