    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10334",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A three input information channel with all three input symbols equally probable, is defined by the channel matrix, \\vert0.50.30.2\\vert \\vert0.20.30.5\\vert \\vert0.30.30.4\\vert Assuming that a maximum-likelihood decision rule is used, determine the error probability, P_E, of the channel.\n\nOptions: \nA. (13) / (30)\nB. (19) / (30)\nC. (15) / (30)\nD. (18) / (30)\nE. (14) / (30)\nF. (17) / (30)\nG. (21) / (30)\nH. (12) / (30)\nI. (16) / (30)\nJ. (20) / (30)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The channel matrix is given as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\n0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\\n0.2 & 0.3 & 0.5 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.3 & 0.4 \\\\\n\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEach row corresponds to the output distribution for a given input. Since inputs are equally probable, each has probability $1/3$. Using the maximum-likelihood (ML) rule, for each output, we decide the input that maximizes the transition probability. We compute the probability of correct decision for each output by selecting the input with the highest probability in each column (since ML chooses the input that makes the observed output most likely), then sum over correct decisions and subtract from 1 to get $P_E$.\n\nLet’s analyze by output:\n\n- Output 0: max at input 0 (0.5) → correct if input was 0\n- Output 1: inputs 0,1,2 have 0.3, 0.3, 0.3 → tie; assume ML breaks in favor of one, but since all equal, choosing any gives 0.3 chance correct when input was that → use 0.3\n- Output 2: max at input 2 (0.5) → correct if input was 2\n\nBut better: compute average error.\n\nInstead, compute total probability of correct decision:\n\nFor each input, compute probability that the output leads to correct ML decision.\n\nBut simpler: since ML rule picks, for each output, the input with largest $P(y|x)$, and inputs are equally likely.\n\nWe do it by output:\n\n- Output 0: $P(y=0|x=0)=0.5$, $P(y=0|x=1)=0.2$, $P(y=0|x=2)=0.3$. So largest is $x=0$ (0.5). Thus, decide $x=0$. Correct only if actual input was 0.\n- Output 1: all $P(y=1|x) = 0.3$ → tie. Assume ML chooses any; in error unless input equals the chosen one. Since inputs equally likely, probability correct given $y=1$ is $1/3$, because only if input is the one chosen (say x=0) and actual was 0 → probability of correct decision given $y=1$: probability that input was the one selected. Since all $P(x)=1/3$, and all $P(y=1|x)=0.3$, so posterior uniform, so $P(\\text{correct}|y=1) = 1/3$. So $P(\\text{error}|y=1) = 2/3$.\n- Output 2: $P(y=2|x=0)=0.2$, $P(y=2|x=1)=0.5$, $P(y=2|x=2)=0.4$ → max at $x=1$ → decide $x=1$. So correct only if input was 1.\n\nNow compute total probability of correct decision:\n\n$$\nP_C = P(y=0) \\cdot P(\\text{correct}|y=0) + P(y=1) \\cdot P(\\text{correct}|y=1) + P(y=2) \\cdot P(\\text{correct}|y=2)\n$$\n\nWe need $P(y)$:\n\n$$\nP(y=0) = \\sum_x P(y=0|x)P(x) = (1/3)(0.5 + 0.2 + 0.3) = (1/3)(1.0) = 1/3\n$$\n\n$$\nP(y=1) = (1/3)(0.3+0.3+0.3) = (1/3)(0.9) = 0.3 = 9/30 = 3/10\n$$\n\nWait: $0.3 = 9/30$, but let's use 30ths:\n\nBetter: use fractions.\n\n$P(x=i)=1/3$ for i=0,1,2.\n\nTransition matrix:\n- Row 0: 0.5, 0.3, 0.2\n- Row 1: 0.2, 0.3, 0.5\n- Row 2: 0.3, 0.3, 0.4\n\nSo:\n\n$P(y=0) = (1/3)(0.5 + 0.2 + 0.3) = (1/3)(1.0) = 1/3$\n\n$P(y=1) = (1/3)(0.3 + 0.3 + 0.3) = (1/3)(0.9) = 0.3 = 3/10$\n\n$P(y=2) = (1/3)(0.2 + 0.5 + 0.4) = (1/3)(1.1) = 1.1/3 = 11/30$\n\nCheck: 1/3 = 10/30, 0.3 = 9/30, 11/30 → sum = (10+9+11)/30 = 30/30 = 1. Good.\n\nNow, $P(\\text{correct}|y=0)$: we decide $x=0$, since $P(y=0|x=0)=0.5$ is max (vs 0.2 and 0.3). So correct if actual $x=0$. By Bayes:\n\n$$\nP(x=0|y=0) = \\frac{P(y=0|x=0)P(x=0)}{P(y=0)} = \\frac{0.5 \\cdot (1/3)}{1/3} = 0.5\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $P(x=2|y=0) = 0.3$, $P(x=1|y=0)=0.2$. So max at $x=0$, so ML chooses $x=0$. So $P(\\text{correct}|\\text{decide }x=0) = P(x=0|y=0) = 0.5$\n\nBut actually, $P(\\text{correct}|y=0)$ is not $P(x=0|y=0)$ unless we know $x=0$. But since we decide $x=0$, the probability that this is correct is $P(x=0|y=0) = 0.5$\n\nWait — but no: when we observe $y=0$, and use ML to decide $\\hat{x}=0$, the probability that this decision is correct is $P(x=0|y=0)$. But we don't know the true $x$. However, for computing average error, we can compute:\n\n$$\nP_C = \\sum_y P(y) \\cdot P(\\text{decide correct input} | y)\n$$\n\nBut $P(\\text{decide correct input} | y) = P(\\hat{x} = x | y)$ averaged over $x$ and decision rule.\n\nActually, better: total $P_C = \\sum_y P(y) \\cdot \\max_x P(x|y) $? No.\n\nNo — since decision is deterministic: for ea